   "gap": "Feller II.9 is selected in full by body lines 11-15 ('II.9, including its continuation', pp. 51-54). The proof, both Robbins bounds, the footnote-17 refinement (in stronger form) and both constant determinations are written, but historical and quantitative claims attached to that presentation are not supplied in the entry (body 101-121) or its annex (112-299): (a) footnote 14 (p. 52, PDF index 66) dates the formula to James Stirling's Methodus differentialis (1730); the route has only the name 'Stirling's formula'; (b) footnote 16 (p. 53, PDF index 67): the usual proof that e^C = sqrt(2 pi) relies on Wallis's formula, and Feller gives a simple direct proof in Amer. Math. Monthly (1967); nothing on the route states either; (c) footnote 15 (p. 53): the argument and inequality (9.14) are due to H. E. Robbins, Amer. Math. Monthly 62 (1955), pp. 26-29; the route keeps the name Robbins but not this source; (d) p. 54 (PDF index 68): the ratio of the extreme members of (9.15) is close to 1 - (12n^2)^{-1}, so the right-hand member overestimates n! with an error of less than 9n^{-2} per cent. State (a)-(d), tying the Wallis remark to the entry's second, cosine-integral determination of the constant, and derive (d) from the proved bounds 1/(12n+1) < r_n < 1/(12n).",
